===============
shell-toolboxes
===============

shell-toolboxes: A set of shell 'toolboxes' with useful functions for daily
tasks in several fields.

 1. DESCRIPTION
================

shell-toolboxes is a small set of useful shell 'toolboxes' to provide handy
functions for daily operations in several contexts.

It's developed and maintained by Agatha Isabelle Moreira and it's likely only
useful for her. Feel free to contribute any changes to the toolboxes or new
toolboxes. Beware that changes will only be accepted if they do not modify
existing behavior according to Agatha's personal needs and preferences, so if
you feel something should be different I strongly encourage you to fork the
project.

 2. INSTALLATION
=================

To install shell-toolboxes, install the `shell-toolboxes.sh` script in the top
of the project tree to a location of your preference and source it in your
bashrc file.

Then create the toolboxes directory:
$ mkdir -p ~/.shell/toolboxes/

Install the scripts in the `toolboxes/` dir to the created toolboxes directory:
$ install toolboxes/* ~/.shell/toolboxes/

 3. USAGE
==========

To load a toolbox, just run the command:
$ load_toolboxes LIST OF TOOLBOXES

Where the LIST OF TOOLBOXES is a list of the specific available toolboxes that
you want to load.

Each toolbox provides its own set of functions and after loading they're
sourced in your shell and you just call the provided functions.

 3.1 Available Toolboxes
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
The following toolboxes are available from this repository:

TOOLBOX NAME	DESCRIPTION
assembly	Useful shell functions when working with assembly
kdev		Useful shell functions to work with linux kernel development
kconf		Useful shell functions to find kernel config options
qemu		Useful shell functions to work with qemu and virtiofsd

 3.2 Extending Toolboxes
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
You can easily add your own toolboxes by just creating a shell script file
written to be sourced making whatever function you want available. You save it
as `toolbox-name.sh` and then load with load_toolboxes toolbox-name to load it.

